<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Progressive hearing loss in both ears over about three years, worse on the left, with constant bilateral tinnitus and intolerance of loud sound, in a 30-year-old professional pianist and composer. Please characterize the loss, look for treatable or retrocochlear causes, and advise on hearing rehabilitation suited to a musician and on managing the tinnitus. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks, with a same-day comprehensive audiologic evaluation. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What type and degree of hearing loss does he have, and is it symmetric?</item><item>Does he need imaging or other tests for a treatable or retrocochlear cause?</item><item>Is he a candidate for hearing aids, and what devices and settings suit a professional musician?</item><item>How should his tinnitus and sensitivity to loud sound be managed?</item><item>What is the outlook, how should his hearing be monitored, and how can he protect what he has?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. van Beethoven, a 30-year-old pianist and composer, is seen at the request of Dr. Vandermeer for progressive hearing loss and tinnitus. He dates the decline to about three years ago, beginning in the left ear. He cannot follow soft speech or group conversation, hears the sounds but not the words of quiet speakers, and misses high notes at a distance. Constant ringing and buzzing in both ears keeps him awake. Loud sound is painful and distorted. He has had no sudden drops, vertigo, otalgia, otorrhea, ear surgery, head injury, or facial weakness, and takes no medicines. He has rehearsed and performed for many years without hearing protection. No family history of early hearing loss is known to him. He is afraid this will end his career as a performer and has kept it secret from colleagues. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ludwig van Beethoven was born in December 1770 in the house at Bonngasse in Bonn. Only his baptism is recorded: he is entered in the register of the parish church of St. Remigius as the first of two children baptized on December 17, 1770. The day of his birth is not recorded; the Beethoven-Haus gives it as probably the 16th or 17th. [D]</item><item>In November 1792 Beethoven left Bonn for Vienna to study with Haydn; he intended to stay a year and lived there for over 33 years. [D]</item><item>His father&apos;s acute alcoholism forced him to retire in 1789. [D]</item><item>In a letter to his friend the physician Franz Gerhard Wegeler dated June 29, 1801, Beethoven wrote that his hearing had become weaker and weaker for the last three years; that a stomach complaint was said to be its cause; that Johann Peter Frank had treated him with tonics and his hearing with oil of almonds, and a tepid Danube bath had helped his general health but not his hearing; that his ears were &quot;ringing and singing night and day&quot;; that for two years he had shunned all society because he could not say to people &quot;I am deaf&quot;; that in the theater he had to lean close to the orchestra to understand the actors and could not hear the high notes of instruments or singers at a distance; that he heard the sounds but not the words of people speaking softly, yet could not bear shouting; and he asked Wegeler to tell no one. [D]</item><item>In a second letter to Wegeler, dated November 16, 1801, he wrote that the ringing in his ears was somewhat less, &quot;especially in my left ear, in which the disease began,&quot; but that his hearing was no better; that his physician, Dr. Vering, had applied blistering bark to both his arms; and that he had begun to mix in society again after two deserted and miserable years. [D]</item><item>Cochlear otosclerosis has been the cause most often proposed by otologists, with Paget disease of bone among the alternatives; a later review notes that the middle ears examined at his autopsy showed no otosclerotic foci. [R]</item><item>Some authors attribute his hearing loss and abdominal complaints to chronic lead poisoning, citing high lead levels in hair and bone, and in 2024 high lead levels were reported in two authenticated locks of his hair; others favor other causes, and the question is not settled. [R]</item><item>A 2023 genome study of authenticated locks of his hair found no genetic explanation for his hearing loss or gastrointestinal problems, but found a genetic predisposition to liver disease and evidence of hepatitis B infection in at least the months before his death; with his alcohol consumption, the authors offered these as plausible explanations for the severe liver disease that culminated in his death. [R]</item><item>His alcohol consumption is described by modern authors as broadly accepted, and his physicians are said to have thought he was dependent on alcohol. [R]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

